/**
 * The `vale` pack (docs/ART.md task S5): the placeholder pack with the slice's art laid over it. The layout comes
 * from the atlas pipeline (layout.ts, generated by `npm run art:build`); the uploaded IDs from assetIds.ts. Each
 * entry switches to its art only when every file it needs has an ID, so with no IDs this pack draws exactly what the
 * placeholder pack draws:
 *   - class.vanguard, enemy.swarmer, enemy.sniper, enemy.bruiser, boss.act1, boss.demonlord: `sprites` once the
 *     sheets of every REQUIRED animation are uploaded, else placeholder; an optional animation (attack.1..3; a boss's
 *     attack.2..4 / intro / taunt) is left out until its sheet is uploaded; a sheet's outline (layout `outlineFile`,
 *     enemy and boss sets) becomes its `outlineAssetId` once uploaded, and never holds the set back; per-sheet cells
 *     are copied;
 *   - tile.biome.1.floor (with tilesPerImage), ui.ring.player, ui.marker.pedestal, ui.marker.boost (the boost drops'
 *     white beam, docs/BOOST_ART.md) and boost.<kind> (the Lords of Pain gold coins / gemstones, with the layout's
 *     pixelsPerTile; their colour role stays manifest.colors[boost.<kind>]): `image` once uploaded, else placeholder
 *     (the ui IDs exist either way; a boost kind keeps its placeholder gem). A boost image needs decor.fx.glow (its halo,
 *     validator rule BA-P2), which comes with decor-atlas.png;
 *   - the floor colour of every biome tiled with tile.biome.1.floor becomes VALE_FLOOR_HEX (the texture's mean colour)
 *     once the texture is uploaded, so the contrast checks describe what is drawn.
 *   - the boon draft's card art (docs/DRAFT_REVEAL.md DR3, contract.ts draftSkinIds: ui.draft.back / .back.eyes /
 *     .card.common / .card.rare / .skull.left / .skull.right, cut from the ToffeeCraft Horror UI sheet): `image` once
 *     uploaded, else ABSENT (optional ids: the overlay draws the flat card), never a placeholder.
 *   - the UI skin (docs/UI_ART.md UA4): the 9-sliced pieces (contract.ts uiSkinIds: ui.panel.menu / .hud / .tall,
 *     ui.button / .focus / .disabled, ...) like the draft art: `image` once uploaded, else ABSENT. The small pieces
 *     (uiSkinIds' sibling uiIconIds: hearts, flasks, chips, glyphs, ...) share ONE icon atlas (uiIconLayout.ts): once
 *     VALE_UI_ICON_FILE has an ID every icon is an `image` of the whole atlas with its `rect` (and `pivot`), else none
 *     is (all or nothing, valeUiIconImages). The required ui.panel / ui.icon.health / .mana / .ember take the art named
 *     in VALE_UI_REQUIRED_ART once it exists, else stay the placeholder.
 * Image pivots and 9-slice insets (VALE_IMAGE_LAYOUTS) go into the ui images' `pivot` / `slice`, icon rects into
 * `rect`; the renderer reads them through ThemePack.image(id).pivot / .slice / .rect.
 *
 * Sounds (docs/FX_AUDIO.md A1, audioLayout.ts generated by `npm run audio:build`): a sound ID switches from the
 * placeholder pack's SILENT to `{ kind: "sound", assetId, variants, volume, pitch, minInterval }` once EVERY one of its
 * built files has an ID (files[0] is the assetId, the rest the variants, in layout order); until then it stays silent,
 * so no sound plays half its variant pool.
 *
 * Biome materials (docs/DECOR.md R1) need no upload, so they apply whatever is in assetIds.ts: VALE_BIOME_MATERIALS,
 * except that a biome whose floor tile is a repeating texture keeps its floor SmoothPlastic (the default, so the
 * floor entry is dropped): a patterned material under the image would fight it.
 *
 * World art (docs/DECOR.md R5): the Lords of Pain props, effects and breakables (VALE_WORLD_LAYOUTS, one shared atlas
 * plus the ambient particle texture), and the pellets' own cubes (VALE_PELLET_ART, docs/PELLETS.md PL8). VALE_DECOR_PIECES / VALE_DECOR_FX / VALE_AMBIENT_ART / VALE_BREAKABLE_ART say
 * which art each ID draws; the same piece serves all five biomes (the pack has no per-biome art). An ID appears only
 * once its file has an ID (decor is optional: absent = not drawn); a breakable stays its placeholder box until then.
 *
 * Sprite effects (fx.ts, docs/FX_AUDIO.md F2, F6): `fx.*` once the effects atlas has an ID. The ambient particle
 * textures (decor.fx.ambient.b<n>) have two sources: the R5 mote (VALE_AMBIENT_ART, every biome) wins once
 * decor-mote.png has an ID; until then a biome whose Ninja Adventure flipbook (fx-ambient-b<n>.png) has an ID uses it.
 */
